NAME
    jsc - assemble strict JavaScript namespace bundles or ordered CSS sources

SYNOPSIS
    jsc <modules.json> [output]

DESCRIPTION
    jsc compiles an ordered JSON tree containing namespace nodes (name/modules)
    and source nodes (file/exports with optional imports). No runtime module
    registry or automatic external-file loading is introduced.

    Earlier declarations are available to later source files in the same
    lexical namespace. Symbols exported in a nested namespace become
    accessible through the corresponding namespace object in parent scopes.

    jsc invokes Node.js from the existing m-integrated launcher. It does not
    install Node.js automatically.

COMPLETE EXAMPLE
    Given these files:

        project/
          modules-js.json
          m/srv/data/MyClass.js
          m/srv/data/Other.js
          m/srv/Facade.js

    Contents of m/srv/data/MyClass.js:

        function MyClass() {
          return 42;
        }

    Contents of m/srv/data/Other.js:

        function Other() {
          return MyClass();
        }

    Contents of m/srv/Facade.js:

        function Facade() {
          return data.MyClass() + data.Other() - 42;
        }

    Contents of modules-js.json (order matters):

        {
          "modules": [
            {
              "name": "m",
              "modules": [
                {
                  "name": "srv",
                  "modules": [
                    {
                      "name": "data",
                      "modules": [
                        {"file": "m/srv/data/MyClass.js", "exports": "MyClass"},
                        {"file": "m/srv/data/Other.js", "exports": "Other"}
                      ]
                    },
                    {"file": "m/srv/Facade.js", "exports": "Facade"}
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }

    Compile to standard output:

        jsc modules-js.json > bundle.js

    Or compile directly to a file:

        jsc modules-js.json bundle.js

    In a browser, load bundle.js:

        <!doctype html>
        <meta charset="utf-8">
        <script src="bundle.js"></script>
        <body>
          <div id="result"></div>
          <script>
            console.log(m.srv.data.MyClass()); // 42
            console.log(m.srv.data.Other());   // 42
            document.getElementById('result').textContent =
              String(m.srv.Facade());
          </script>

    The result is 42. The standalone classic script creates namespace m,
    m.srv and m.srv.data as it executes. In the data namespace, MyClass is
    directly usable by later source files; in m.srv, data.MyClass is usable;
    qualified access through m.srv.data.MyClass is also available.

MODULES.JSON FORMAT
    The root object contains an ordered "modules" array. Optional Boolean
    "nodeGlobal" defaults to false and controls global publication in Node.

    A namespace node:
        name       namespace name: one valid JavaScript identifier
        modules    nested ordered array of namespace and source nodes

    A source node:
        file       relative source pathname
        exports    optional comma-separated exported identifiers
        imports    optional object mapping local names to namespace paths

    Example of multiple exports:

        {"file": "m/util/clock.js", "exports": "clock,clockUTC"}

    Example of a side-effect-only source:

        {"file": "m/init.js", "exports": ""}

    The files are included in JSON order. jsc does not automatically sort
    them, discover references or build a dependency graph. The project
    author chooses order. Source paths are relative to the descriptor
    directory and may not escape it. Invalid identifiers and JavaScript
    syntax are rejected.

    Namespace objects can be reopened by a subsequently loaded bundle.
    Previous exports remain accessible through qualified namespace paths.
    If an unqualified alias is needed, imports generates a lexical reference:

        {"file": "m/srv/Extension.js",
         "imports": {"A": "m.data.A"}, "exports": "Extension"}

    Equivalent source code may declare: var A = m.data.A;
    Imports never load or embed provider source files. Captured references
    retain their value if a namespace property is later replaced.

NODE.JS AND BROWSER
    Generated JavaScript uses an outer strict closure with nested namespace
    closures. Browser classic scripts publish namespace roots on globalThis.

    Node CommonJS normally publishes the root namespace through
    module.exports only, without reading or writing globalThis.m. Set
    "nodeGlobal": true at the descriptor root to reuse or create the shared
    global namespace in Node; module.exports returns the same object.
    Multiple independently compiled bundles must be loaded in dependency
    order before acquiring references; no import loads a bundle.

CSS
    A CSS manifest is another ordered source list, without namespaces:

        {
          "modules": [
            {"file": "css/reset.css"},
            {"file": "css/layout.css"}
          ]
        }

    Example:

        jsc modules-css.json styles.css

    Concatenation respects input order. JavaScript and CSS belong in
    different manifests and are not mixed inside one assembly.

OUTPUT AND RELEASE ARTIFACTS
    With one operand, source bytes are emitted only to stdout. Redirect
    this stream to produce your file; no additional files are created.

    With an explicit output filename, jsc writes the assembled file.
    It additionally produces gzip output. When an already-installed
    compatible minifier is available, it produces <name>.min.js or
    <name>.min.css and the corresponding .gz of that minified file.

    JavaScript minification uses an installed google-closure-compiler.
    CSS minification uses an installed uglifycss. If the minifier is
    absent, the raw assembly remains usable and its .gz is produced
    with a warning. jsc does not install tools or access the network.

    JavaScript syntax is validated by Node before emission. If Closure
    Compiler is available, jsc also runs its non-mutating --checks_only
    validation before emitting source. The launcher retains its existing
    redirection behavior; output replacement on failure is not guaranteed
    to be atomic.

ERRORS
    The launcher reports fatal jsc.<branch> diagnostics. The Node engine
    reports branch-specific errors to stderr and exits nonzero.

    Engine statuses:
         1   Invalid arguments
         2   Descriptor, source or generated syntax invalid
         3   Installed Closure Compiler check failed
         4   Output write failed

    Launcher statuses:
         1   Missing/invalid operands
         2   Compiler execution failed
         4   Installed minifier failed
         5   Gzip generation failed

FILES
    <jsc-package-root>/bin/jsc
    <jsc-package-root>/lib/jsc.lib.js
    <jsc-package-root>/res/manual/jsc

ENVIRONMENT
    Requires a nonempty m_ROOT inherited from an initialized m environment.
    From an uninitialized host shell, invoke m jsc [arguments].


